ery good job i think often  were too caught up in the daily story  we're too much attention is given to how  can we move the story ahead on a 24 hour  cycle and journalism gives a lot of  attention to trying to describe what's  going to happen political pundits will  sit around and say who are the democrats  going to nominate in 92 who's going to  win will bush keep quail on the ticket  all kinds of speculative things and then  there are a whole group of journalists  and pundits who will sit around and say  this is what should have happened the  democrats instead of nominating Dukakis  should have nominated so-and-so Dukakis  should have one run his campaign the  following way I think journalism is best  not one that's trying to figure out  what's going to happen or what should  have happened but when it tries to  figure out what did happen and that's  hard and it takes time and no source or  a group of sources is going to fully  describe it to you and so I tend to  spend a long time often too long on  these books or stories for the paper  because as soon as you get a hold of  that sock on the thread and start  pulling it you realize that it's going  to take a long time and that there's a  lot of thread in a lot of sock as you  know you've gotten some criticism for  not revealing some of the information  this book during the time that you're  writing in other words as a reporter and  having the post there as an outlet that  it would have been in any given time  interesting to the whole debate if we'd  known some of this what's your answer to  that first of all well that you can't  get it unless you make an agreement with  the sources that it goes that is for the  book and the book exclusively a couple  of times I did stories for the post went  back to sources and said I think we  should describe some of these things and  I did if I'd really learn something  startling if I'd learned that say Cheney  or Powell opposed the war I really  thought it was a mistake but it 